  'body' => <<<'HTML'
<h2>Why Low Voltage Is the Standard for Landscape Lighting</h2>
<p>Low voltage landscape lighting Austin homeowners install today runs at 12 volts, stepped down from household 120 volts by a transformer. That single decision shapes everything else about the system. Twelve volts cannot deliver a dangerous shock, so cable can be buried at shallow depth in planting beds without conduit, fixtures can be touched by children and pets, and a fixture can be moved three feet to the left when the agave grows without calling an electrician. Modern LED lamps have made 12V systems efficient enough that a 30-fixture property draws less power than a hair dryer.</p>
<p>Austin Landscape Lighting has installed more than 500 low-voltage systems since 2020. Because the fixtures get all the attention and the wiring is buried, the quality of the underlying system is where good contractors and poor ones differ most. This page explains what we do beneath the surface.</p>

<h2>Components of a Properly Built System</h2>
<h3>Transformer</h3>
<p>The transformer converts 120V to 12V and houses the photocell and astronomical timer. We install stainless steel units with multiple voltage taps, sized so the connected LED load falls between roughly 40 and 80 percent of capacity. Undersized transformers run hot; grossly oversized ones can cause flicker with some LED drivers and waste money. The unit mounts at least 12 inches above grade on a wall near a GFCI-protected outdoor outlet, in shade where possible. For larger properties we use two or three transformers near the fixture groups rather than one giant unit, which keeps cable runs short and troubleshooting simple.</p>
<h3>Cable</h3>
<p>Direct-burial low-voltage cable in 12 gauge is our standard, with 10 gauge for long home runs. Cable is trenched 6 inches deep in beds and deeper where it crosses turf, and sleeved in conduit under driveways, walks and anywhere a shovel is likely. Voltage drop is the enemy: a long run of undersized cable leaves the last fixtures dim and yellow. We design every run so the farthest fixture sees within one volt of the nearest.</p>
<h3>Wiring method</h3>
<p>There are three ways to connect fixtures to a transformer, and we choose based on the layout:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hub method.</strong> A home run from the transformer to a central waterproof hub, with short equal leads to each fixture. Every fixture sees the same voltage, which keeps LED color consistent. Our default for groups of 4 to 8 fixtures.</li>
<li><strong>Loop (T) method.</strong> Cable runs out along a line of fixtures and back to the transformer, feeding both ends. Good for long path runs.</li>
<li><strong>Daisy chain.</strong> Fixtures connected in series along one cable. Simple, but the last fixture always gets less voltage. We use it only for very short runs.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Connections</h3>
<p>Every splice uses a gel-filled, waterproof connector rated for direct burial. We do not use pierce-point clips or indoor twist-on connectors, which are the number one cause of the repair calls we take on other companies' systems. Connections are placed in accessible spots near fixtures and documented on your plan.</p>
<h3>Fixtures</h3>
<p>Solid brass or copper bodies with 2700K LED lamps or integrated LED modules rated for enclosed fixtures and high ambient temperature. Brass develops a dark patina within a year and then lasts for decades. Aluminum pits and plastic cracks in Austin heat, so we do not install them.</p>

