<h2>Austin-Specific Installation Notes</h2>
<p><strong>Caliche and rock.</strong> Trenching in Central Texas is slow and sometimes requires a rock bar or trenching machine. Where rock is shallow we route cable along bed edges and under mulch, protected by conduit where depth cannot be achieved.</p>
<p><strong>Irrigation.</strong> Nearly every Austin bed has drip or spray irrigation. We locate lines before trenching and keep lighting cable separated from irrigation so future repairs to either do not damage the other.</p>
<p><strong>Heat.</strong> Transformers mount in shade and fixtures use drivers rated for high ambient temperatures. Cheap electronics fail in their second Austin summer.</p>
<p><strong>Tree roots.</strong> Live oak and cedar elm roots are shallow and valuable. Cable near trunks is hand-dug and routed around roots rather than through them.</p>
<p><strong>Dark-sky and HOA.</strong> Low-voltage LED fixtures are inherently low output and easy to shield, which is why a well-designed 12V system is the dark-sky friendly choice compared with 120V floods. We specify 2700K throughout and provide layouts for HOA review when requested.</p>

<h2>Installation Day and Beyond</h2>
<p>After your free on-site design consultation and written, no-surprise estimate, licensed and insured technicians arrive with the fixtures, transformer and cable for the full system. A typical day runs: mark irrigation and utilities, mount the transformer, trench and lay cable, set hubs and connections, place and wire fixtures, power up and test voltage at every fixture, back-fill and restore mulch and turf. Most residential systems are complete by late afternoon, and we return after dark to aim each fixture and set the timer. Every system includes a 2-year workmanship warranty from Austin Landscape Lighting plus manufacturer warranties on fixtures and the transformer. The planning range above is a planning figure, not a quote.</p>

<h2>Build It Right Underneath</h2>
